/-!
# Adjoining an algebraic constant does not enlarge the span

Theorem statement: `IsIntegral.mem_span_of_adjoin_simple_constants`
(`334cf788-7019-5491-894a-0eed8ed1f5c0`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `8f2cfe2c-1483-570c-afcf-afd985706bbd`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra structure, let $F$ be an intermediate field
of $\mathbb{C}/\mathbb{Q}$, and let $B \subseteq S$ be subsets of $K$. Let $c \in \mathbb{C}$ be
algebraic over $F$, and write $F(c)$ for the intermediate field of $\mathbb{C}/F$ generated by $c$,
viewed as a subset of $\mathbb{C}$. Let $y \in K$ satisfy two hypotheses: first, $y$ belongs to the
subfield of $K$ generated by the image of $F(c)$ under the structure map $\mathbb{C} \to K$ together
with $S$; second, $y$ is integral over the subring of $K$ generated by the image of $F(c)$ under the
structure map together with $B$. The conclusion is that $y$ lies in the $\mathbb{C}$-submodule of
$K$ spanned by the set of those $z \in K$ which simultaneously belong to the subfield of $K$
generated by the image of $F$ together with $S$ and are integral over the subring of $K$ generated
by the image of $F$ together with $B$.

This is the algebraic-constant step in the descent of the field of constants: enlarging the
coefficient field $F$ inside $\mathbb{C}$ by a single algebraic number leaves unchanged the
$\mathbb{C}$-span of the elements of $K$ that are $F$-rational in the above sense and integral over
$F[B]$. It is used in the $q$-expansion argument, where it is cited by
[`WLight.frickeFunction_intBaseChange`](thm.html#WLight.frickeFunction_intBaseChange).

